{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox","id":1558454,"title":"Extended rolls and paradox","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:25:52-08:00","id":1558454,"title":"Extended rolls and paradox","content":"In M20, the rules for Rituals are quite clear, but when it comes to a non ritual extended roll (&quot;picking up where you left off&quot;), the rules are not clear at all to me.<br /> <br /> So, under this mode, how are difficult and paradox ruled? Let's say you're trying for a 15 successes spell with Arete 3 and not using a ritual.<br /> <br /> Your diff would increase at each roll, but would it increase in failed rolls (as in 0 successea that turn) too? What about paradox?<br /> <br /> Bonus question: a failed vulgar spell grants 1 paradox too? I know this is true for older editions, but M20 seems to point only for successful vulgar spells granting paradox (and any spell botching).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-23T06:46:21-08:00","id":1558467,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your diff would increase at each roll, but would it increase in failed rolls (as in 0 successea that turn) too? </div> </div> </div>Yes, but due to Failed Rolls rule. Essentially to &quot;keep going&quot; and to &quot;fail a roll&quot; it's functionally the same on these cases. But you can't keep going if you botch, it's not a ritual.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> a failed vulgar spell grants 1 paradox too? </div> </div> </div> It's as you say, failed spells don't generate Paradox.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What about paradox? </div> </div> </div> I would think: Nothing until you either cast the spell or botch. Unlike with rituals, it doesn't increase per roll.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-23T08:04:22-08:00","id":1558469,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox#post1558454\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In M20, the rules for Rituals are quite clear, but when it comes to a non ritual extended roll (&quot;picking up where you left off&quot;), the rules are not clear at all to me.<br /> <br /> So, under this mode, how are difficult and paradox ruled? Let's say you're trying for a 15 successes spell with Arete 3 and not using a ritual.<br /> <br /> Your diff would increase at each roll, but would it increase in failed rolls (as in 0 successea that turn) too? What about paradox?<br /> <br /> Bonus question: a failed vulgar spell grants 1 paradox too? I know this is true for older editions, but M20 seems to point only for successful vulgar spells granting paradox (and any spell botching).</div> </div> </div> </div>So to start, the way M20 describes it, at least in the section I am looking at (Magical Feats, M20, pg 537) a ritual is just the term for an extended casting roll. The implication is that if your character is intentionally casting over several terms, you use ritual rules. Your Arete 3, 15 success example, then, is a ritual since there's no reasonable way to get 15 successes on a single Arete 3 roll (odds of getting that through exploding dice is around the odds of winning two major lotteries back to back, give or take an order of magnitude).<br /> <br /> But in the case where the attempt was to cast in a single round and that attempt fell short, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a> is right, both failed rolls and partial rolls incur a +1 going forward, which is why the book suggests that if you get two of those in a row its a good time to intentionally cast as an extended roll. Paradox would also not accumulate with each roll, that's a ritual thing.<br /> <br /> Now as you point out, M20 doesn't have a rule for failed magic of any type generating paradox, which is odd because M20 also says that Vulgar magic ALWAYS accumulates paradox. And M20 is the one that put that in all caps. But whatever the case, as written no paradox is incurred on either kind of extended spell until it succeeds or there is a botch.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-24T16:02:13-08:00","id":1558499,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a> , That's how I alwaysnthought rhisnworked too, but I am confused now that I gave a bit of thought about it:<br /> What counts as a failed roll? Getting 0 successes on a roll or not getting the required amount? Let's use a simpler example, let's say the mage wants a spell that requires 4 successes.<br /> He rolls Arete (3) and gets 2 successes. That's not a failed roll as it is (wasn't 0 successes) but he also didn't complete the spell. As per the degree of successes table, he can keep trying at +1 diff, right?<br /> <br /> My issue with the paradox thing is the contradictions we have in p. 501 where there are two word in parenthesis &quot;(Take Paradox)&quot; in step four when it ask if you failed (I believe this is not a roll, but the whole effect). HOWEVER, in the paradox section it is pretty clear that only <b>successful</b> vulgar spells or botches generate paradox, so which on is right?<br /> <br /> And when it comes to extended rolls, in page 538 (the &quot;Picking up where youbleft off&quot; rule) it says<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For situations in which you try again due to a failed roll \u2013 or try again and fail the roll \u2013 check out the Failed Rolls entry below </div> </div> </div>Does this means that this rule works like rituals when you fail (0 successes) a roll, as in, you get +1 diff AND accumulate 1 paradox for each failed roll (which goes away if you succed)??? This is so confusing\u2026<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> , It seems to be a difference between a ritual and a &quot;picking up&quot; cast. Rituals take a lot more time and do not increase diff unless you get 0 successes in a roll, while &quot;picking up&quot; always increase diff, but it takes a LOT less time and preparation.<br /> <br /> I understand that rituals have to be announced beforehand (you can't just turn a regular casting into a ritual). But what you're saying is that the required amount of successes needed is higher than the mage's Arete, you can't try to cast it without a ritual and that's not something I see written in raw\u2026<br /> <br /> So, what you guys think about that and the raw I provided?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-25T08:10:39-08:00","id":1558509,"title":"","content":"No, there's no rule stating that if something is too hard you can't attempt it as a simple action. I'm saying that the odds are so bad that it simply doesn't make sense to even try. The player can't reasonably believe he'll accomplish the roll in a single roll and the character can't reasonably believe he can manage that complex a spell so quickly. You technically can use the Simple Roll rules in this case, but it doesn't represent what is going on in the game so why would you?<br /> <br /> To be clear, you made this a very easy distinction with your example. The odds of getting 15 successes with a dice pool of 3 is around 1 in 1,000,000,000,000,000 (give or take a few zeros). If I were to make a roll once a second for the next 10 million years the odds are still against me ever getting that result.<br /> <br /> For a more reasonable example, lets use your other example where the mage needs 4 successes and has Arete of 3. On a Difficulty 6 roll, that's around a 1 in 20 chance for a single roll. Now we are into the territory where in most cases we would handle this as an extended roll (and thus a ritual) but there are still cases like desperation or limited window of opportunity where a mage might try to cast more quickly and thus use the simple casting rules. Which one we choose will depend on what is going on in the narrative.<br /> <br /> As for your other questions:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558499#post1558499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What counts as a failed roll? Getting 0 successes on a roll or not getting the required amount? Let's use a simpler example, let's say the mage wants a spell that requires 4 successes.<br /> <br /> He rolls Arete (3) and gets 2 successes. That's not a failed roll as it is (wasn't 0 successes) but he also didn't complete the spell. As per the degree of successes table, he can keep trying at +1 diff, right?</div> </div> </div> </div>A failed roll is one with no successes. The scenario you gave is not a failed roll, the mage can continue at a +1 difficulty<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">My issue with the paradox thing is the contradictions we have in p. 501 where there are two word in parenthesis &quot;(Take Paradox)&quot; in step four when it ask if you failed (I believe this is not a roll, but the whole effect). HOWEVER, in the paradox section it is pretty clear that only <b>successful</b> vulgar spells or botches generate paradox, so which on is right?</div> </div> </div> </div>I pointed this out last time, it looks like it was intended that failed vulgar rolls should also get Paradox, but there are no rules to impose it. <br /> <br /> [quote = Lord Revan]Does this means that this rule works like rituals when you fail (0 successes) a roll, as in, you get +1 diff AND accumulate 1 paradox for each failed roll (which goes away if you succeed)??? This is so confusing\u2026[/quote]<br /> <br /> No, for several reasons.<br /> <br /> First, in rituals the Potential Paradox isn't a price of failure. Rituals gather Potential Paradox with each new roll, regardless of failure.<br /> <br /> Second, because the section you quoted isn't about continuing to roll after a partial success, that was the previous paragraph. Instead it is about trying again after a failure. That's also a situation with a +1 difficulty, but it is a separate attempt, not a continuing one.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-05T10:10:57-08:00","id":1558664,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558509#post1558509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I pointed this out last time, it looks like it was intended that failed vulgar rolls should also get Paradox, but there are no rules to impose it.</div> </div> </div> </div>The only reason that this seems a bit intended was because of revised edition that worked this way. M20 does a terrible job even hinting that way.<br /> Out of curiosity, how dors your table rule that?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558509#post1558509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, for several reasons.<br /> <br /> First, in rituals the Potential Paradox isn't a price of failure. Rituals gather Potential Paradox with each new roll, regardless of failure.</div> </div> </div> </div>But you only get it if you fail. Anyway, I don't know exactly what has to do with the question\u2026<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558509#post1558509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Second, because the section you quoted isn't about continuing to roll after a partial success, that was the previous paragraph. Instead it is about trying again after a failure. That's also a situation with a +1 difficulty, but it is a separate attempt, not a continuing one.</div> </div> </div> </div>Aren't both paragraphs under the &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot; section? Wdym by separate attempt?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-05T14:37:23-08:00","id":1558669,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558664#post1558664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The only reason that this seems a bit intended was because of revised edition that worked this way. M20 does a terrible job even hinting that way.<br /> Out of curiosity, how dors your table rule that?</div> </div> </div> </div>That and because M20 says that vulgar magic always gets paradox.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20, pg 547</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Beyond that, though, vulgar magick ALWAYS accumulates Paradox, even when the roll succeeds</div> </div> </div> </div>But I can't say I've ever implemented a house rule to make this happen.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But you only get it if you fail. Anyway, I don't know exactly what has to do with the question\u2026 </div> </div> </div>My point was that Potentia-dox is accrued based on how long the ritual goes on, regardless of the success or failure of each roll. So there's no reason that a pick up roll would get it on a failure when it doesn't on a success.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Aren't both paragraphs under the &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot; section? Wdym by separate attempt? </div> </div> </div>Yes, but the first paragraph is specifically about continuing after an imperfect success or falling short. That is, the mage got some successes but either not enough for the full effect (imperfect success) or not enough on an all or nothing roll (falling short). Here the wording is about &quot;picking up where she left off&quot; which implies keeping successes.<br /> <br /> The second paragraph is instead about a failed roll, that is no successes. And here the wording is about &quot;trying again.&quot; That's the same wording as the section on failed mundane rolls on pg 392. There's no carryover.<br /> <br /> Now there's room for ST interpretation here but to me it looks like on simple spell rolls (as opposed to rituals) failure ends any attempts to pick up where you left off. If it was an all or nothing spell the mage is back to zero successes. If it was an imperfect success, such as a healing, some of the effect comes through, but the mage has to make a separate attempt for any more. So there's no Potentia-dox on a failure here because the attempt is over.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-05T15:44:12-08:00","id":1558670,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558669#post1558669\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My point was that Potentia-dox is accrued based on how long the ritual goes on, regardless of the success or failure of each roll. So there's no reason that a pick up roll would get it on a failure when it doesn't on a success.</div> </div> </div> </div>My point is that pick up rule might work exactly like rituals with the exception that it increase diff regardless of a failed or successful roll at the benefit of requiring a lot less time (a few turns).<br /> <br /> I might be wrong, though. I'm really trying to make sense of the raw.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558669#post1558669\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, but the first paragraph is specifically about continuing after an imperfect success or falling short. That is, the mage got some successes but either not enough for the full effect (imperfect success) or not enough on an all or nothing roll (falling short). Here the wording is about &quot;picking up where she left off&quot; which implies keeping successes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, here the 1st paragraph raw on page 537:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> After casting an imperfect Effect or falling short of her initial plans, a mage can pick up where she left off by making another roll at +1 to the original difficulty. In story terms, she grits her teeth, regroups, and gives it the old (wizard) college try once again. </div> </div> </div>It is not crystal clear what &quot;falling short&quot; means. 0 successes are indeed falling short. And in page 504 table we have this text:<br /> <br /> <i>Total Failure: No successes. Effect falls short. Mage can try again at +1 difficulty.</i><br /> <br /> Seems to me that falling short might mean a total fail as well\u2026<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558669#post1558669\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The second paragraph is instead about a failed roll, that is no successes. And here the wording is about &quot;trying again.&quot; That's the same wording as the section on failed mundane rolls on pg 392. There's no carryover.</div> </div> </div> </div>Here's the second paragraph:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For situations in which you try again due to a failed roll \u2013 or try again and fail the roll \u2013 check out the Failed Rolls entry below. In other cases, an extended roll might be in order\u2026 and for those situations, again see the Rituals, Rolls, and Extended Successes entry below </div> </div> </div>Ok, so until now 1st paragraph seem to say that if you get less than the required successes (including 0), you can try again, ok.<br /> <br /> Now, if you tried again due to a failed roll (0 successes) or tried again and got a failed roll, then you should check the Failed Rolls section. Which is the rituals section\u2026<br /> <br /> Then as long as you roll at least 1 success you get no potential paradox. And if you fail a roll you get? While rituals always get regardless on hownmany successes? Maybe that's it? Thisnmakes picking up better than rituals, though, don't it?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558669#post1558669\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now there's room for ST interpretation here but to me it looks like on simple spell rolls (as opposed to rituals) failure ends any attempts to pick up where you left off. If it was an all or nothing spell the mage is back to zero successes. If it was an imperfect success, such as a healing, some of the effect comes through, but the mage has to make a separate attempt for any more. So there's no Potentia-dox on a failure here because the attempt is over.</div> </div> </div> </div>Failure does not end future attempts, thisnis clear in page 504 and on the 2nd paragraph that says: if you fail, check Failed Rolls (which allows to continue).<br /> <br /> Honestly, this is still very confusing to me\u2026","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-06T05:00:11-08:00","id":1558677,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558670#post1558670\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, if you tried again due to a failed roll (0 successes) or tried again and got a failed roll, then you should check the Failed Rolls section. Which is the rituals section\u2026<br /> <br /> Then as long as you roll at least 1 success you get no potential paradox. And if you fail a roll you get? While rituals always get regardless on hownmany successes? Maybe that's it? Thisnmakes picking up better than rituals, though, don't it?</div> </div> </div> </div>No, you're specificly guided towards the Failed Rolls section. The &quot;potential paradox&quot; it's on &quot;Rituals and Paradox&quot; section, and since this isn't a ritual and nothing on the Failed Rolls section points towards there then you gain no &quot;potential paradox&quot; at all when &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot;<br /> <br /> Even when you Fail while &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot;, you wouldn't gain &quot;potential paradox&quot;. If anything, you would gain <i>real</i> Paradox (actual Paradox that doesn't go away if you succeed), if we interpret the rules as Failures giving you Paradox. But I'm unconvinced this is the case on M20<br /> <br /> This makes picking up better than rituals?. Well, maybe if it's something you would be able to cast on very few rolls. Otherwise the chance of Failing it's higher. And it also increases the chances of Botching, and on a Botch it's explicit that you can't keep going.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It is not crystal clear what &quot;falling short&quot; means. 0 successes are indeed falling short. </div> </div> </div>On this system, Failure it's a reserved word that's used for rolls that have no successes (either by not rolling any success, or by having those being cancelled by ones). While not getting enough successes to perform an action could be understood as &quot;failing&quot; on common parlance, it's not really Failing as this system understands it.<br /> <br /> So, &quot;falling short&quot; means not being successful on a common parlance. Failing it's a reserved word that means getting 0 successes. It's similar, but not always the same - in this case Failure invokes a special rule and takes you out of the &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot; section.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 03-06-2026, 08:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-06T07:24:15-08:00","id":1558679,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558677#post1558677\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No, you're specificly guided towards the Failed Rolls section. The &quot;potential paradox&quot; it's on &quot;Rituals and Paradox&quot; section, and since this isn't a ritual and nothing on the Failed Rolls section points towards there then you gain no &quot;potential paradox&quot; at all when &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing is that it points you to the Rituals and Paradox section at the end of the text:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Failed Rolls</b><br /> <br /> Aside from the potential benefits of coincidence (shown above), a ritual\u2019s primary purpose in game terms involves the ability to use extended rolls to create bigger Effects. On the good side, an extended roll can help you assemble a large number of successes; on the not-so-good side, it also gives you plenty of opportunities to fail or botch a roll. Each new roll presents a risk of failure and potential catastrophe. If you fail a roll \u2013 that is, you get no successes that turn \u2013 then you may still continue rolling in subsequent turns. Each failed roll, however, adds +1 to the difficulty of those subsequent rolls. Fail one roll, it\u2019s +1; fail two rolls, and it\u2019s +2\u2026 and so it goes until you either complete the Effect, fail completely, or botch a roll. If the difficulty reaches 9 and you fail another roll and acquire a new penalty, then the new penalties become thresholds: a +1 difficulty adds one more success to the total you need, a +2 difficulty adds two more successes, and so on. By that point, your mage is risking disaster, and it\u2019s probably best to stop the process and regroup than it is to press forward \u2013 see Rituals and Paradox, below </div> </div> </div>Also note that this text talks about rituals. So, either picking up are being considered rituals, or they just didn't noticed/bothered to correct that text so it would apply to picking up.<br /> <br /> Another thing I dont get is what exactly this text adds to the picking up rule (since it already increases diff by roll), why did it tell you to check this section?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558677#post1558677\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even when you Fail while &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot;, you wouldn't gain &quot;potential paradox&quot;. If anything, you would gain <i>real</i> Paradox (actual Paradox that doesn't go away if you succeed), if we interpret the rules as Failures giving you Paradox. But I'm unconvinced this is the case on M20</div> </div> </div> </div>I was unconvinced too, but that last bit quited by Ramnesis seems to bring a bit of clarity to that? Do you havr any arguments against it?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558677#post1558677\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This makes picking up better than rituals?. Well, maybe if it's something you would be able to cast on very few rolls. Otherwise the chance of Failing it's higher. And it also increases the chances of Botching, and on a Botch it's explicit that you can't keep going.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, not if you reduce difficulty. Honestly I think picking up is better than rituals (independently of the paradox rules) just because diff reductions are abundant and how long rituals normally take (even worst with the optional rules).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558677#post1558677\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On this system, Failure it's a reserved word that's used for rolls that have no successes (either by not rolling any success, or by having those being cancelled by ones). While not getting enough successes to perform an action could be understood as &quot;failing&quot; on common parlance, it's not really Failing as this system understands it.<br /> <br /> So, &quot;falling short&quot; means not being successful on a common parlance. Failing it's a reserved word that means getting 0 successes. It's similar, but not always the same - in this case Failure invokes a special rule and takes you out of the &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot; section.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a very important distiction, yeah. Ok, which rule does failure invokes, then? The same as rituals? What if you fail a roll and then succeed at another?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-06T08:51:38-08:00","id":1558682,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558670#post1558670\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Failure does not end future attempts, thisnis clear in page 504 and on the 2nd paragraph that says: if you fail, check Failed Rolls (which allows to continue).<br /> <br /> Honestly, this is still very confusing to me\u2026</div> </div> </div> </div>I really don\u2019t know why it even points to that section. Firstly, the failed rolls section is explicitly about rituals. Even if it wasn\u2019t, all it says is that after a failure the difficulty goes up by 1, which the Picking Up section already said, and if the failure goes above 9 we should use the Thresholds rule, which is actually an optional rule for all rolls with a difficulty above 9. There\u2019s nothing new here.<br /> <br /> Rituals and Paradox is explicitly about rituals and only about rituals. You can apply Potentia-dox to simple rolls if you want, but its a house rule, and the effect is to make picking up where you left off even more risky.<br /> <br /> But maybe it would be helpful to summarize what I see the rules saying:<br /> <br /> For regular non-casting rolls there are simple rolls and extended rolls. Simple rolls are things that can easily accomplished in a short period of time. A single success is enough to pass, even if more would be better. If you don\u2019t get at least that single success, you can often try again at a +1 difficulty.<br /> <br /> Extended rolls are more complicated tasks that require a set number of successes, known in advance. They tend to take longer for a roll than simple rolls though this is largely a matter of what is dramatically appropriate; It is possible to have an extended roll in the middle of a pitched battle. Failed rolls during an extended roll do not raise the difficulty of continuing, nor do they take away successes already gathered.<br /> <br /> Now spells are similar - there are still simple rolls and extended rolls (rituals). But there are two complications we need to deal with. First, even seemingly simple spells often require more than one success, and we don\u2019t want to make every spell that requires more than one success a ritual. Second, big magic should probably risk more Paradox, but rituals tend to be much safer than simple rolls so we need a way of making them carry more risk.<br /> <br /> So the changes the book makes for casting are:<br /> <br /> Spells that require more than one success can still be cast as simple rolls. But since one success may not be enough there is now a safety net: simple spells can be continued if they don\u2019t get enough successes but the price is that the difficulty goes up fast: +1 difficulty for each roll after the first, regardless of if any successes were rolled or not. It\u2019s an ST call whether successes can be kept through a failed roll. Simple spell rolls use standard paradox rules.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, rituals follow the normal extended rolling rules with a few significant changes. First, unlike regular extended rolls, each ritual roll with no successes brings a +1 difficulty on all future rolls. Second, every roll after the first increases the potential paradox of the spell by 1. There are a few other changes but these are the main ones we are discussing here. These rules make rituals a more reliable way of approaching big magic, while still increasing the paradox on a catastrophic failure.<br /> <br /> Like with non-spell rolls, simple spell rolls tend to take a shorter amount of time than each roll in a ritual, but ultimately the ST should choose the time a roll takes by what is dramatically appropriate.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-06T09:30:57-08:00","id":1558683,"title":"","content":"<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Failed Rolls</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">By that point, <b>your mage is risking disaster, and it\u2019s probably</b> best to stop the process and regroup than it is to press forward \u2013 see Rituals and Paradox, below</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, that text it's more of a recomendation than a rule, it says that maybe it could end in disaster, and then cualifies what disaster means by mentioning a rule below that applies to rituals specificly.<br /> <br /> Personaly, I think that it was a small orientation mistake. The text it's on the ritual section, and it's entirely possible the author forgot that there was a reference on <i>another </i>section the reader could be comming from.<br /> <br /> The phrase it's still generally true: You risk Botching, that &quot; is risking disaster, and it\u2019s probably best to stop the process&quot; in my opinion. Botching it's way worse than Failing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Another thing I dont get is what exactly this text adds to the picking up rule (since it already increases diff by roll), why did it tell you to check this section? </div> </div> </div>I honestly tought it was a waste of words and the time of the reader.<br /> <br /> Either that, or is what @<b><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\">Ramnesis</a></b> said: these aren't extended rolls and so you can &quot;keep going&quot; as long as you don't fail, but if you fail the ST can choose not to let you pick it up anymore.... but it's not clear at all.<br /> <br /> Or maybe you're rigth and &quot;Pick it up&quot; are intended to be extended rolls (using all the rules of rituals) with the only difference being that these are <i>unintended</i> (you only do it when you &quot;fall short&quot;, RAI that suggests you should have been able to succeed), and thus increase the difficulty on each roll even on success.<br /> <br /> But that's not clear at all either, AND the section itself seems to treat &quot;Pick it up&quot; and rituals as distinct. And doesn't mesh very well with the (admitedly optional) rule about Great Works (that would limit &quot;Pick Me&quot; spells to 5 successes at most)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-07T09:46:05-08:00","id":1558704,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a>, your take is the one I had before, except for the possibility of keeping the successes, the way I view it, they are certainly kept. Makes no sense otherwise.<br /> Still, while I agree with your analysis, I'd like to get to this conclusion based on the raw text. Particularly the quotes I gave from M20 in my last posts. Especially the second paragraph.<br /> <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In other cases, an extended roll might be in order\u2026 and for those situations, again see the Rituals, Rolls, and Extended Successes entry below </div> </div> </div>This text makes it clear that picking up does not count as an extended roll, nor ritual.<br /> <br /> Which can only mean that that 2nd paragraph is indeed an error from the author. Every bit of text that is pointed by it is concerning rituals\u2026<br /> <br /> I hate how irresponsible Brucato was with the mechanics and rules in this book\u2026<br /> <br /> So, if picking up works like a regular casting except that your difficulty is increasing, as in it grants only 1 paradox, it takes one turn per roll and if you botch your spell fizzle, doesn't this still makes it way better than rituals as long as you can deal with the difficulty?<br /> <br /> I mean, why would you spend several minutes, hours or days in a casting when you can do it within turns? Even big spells like 10+ successes can be done like that when you can deal with the diff increase.<br /> <br /> And better yet, it's only 1 paradox","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-07T11:50:32-08:00","id":1558705,"title":"","content":"Yes the mechanics have some real holes. Though I don\u2019t know if the issue was that they were badly written from the start or if they changed part way through the writing process and not all the old text was caught in editing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558704#post1558704\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I mean, why would you spend several minutes, hours or days in a casting when you can do it within turns? Even big spells like 10+ successes can be done like that when you can deal with the diff increase.<br /> <br /> And better yet, it's only 1 paradox</div> </div> </div> </div>If you don\u2019t botch, yes. Same for the ritual. Though each difficulty increase makes botching more likely and trying to cast as a simple roll and picking up goes up much faster.<br /> <br /> But also look at the difficulty modifiers for magic. There are several reductions, like taking extra time and research and preparation, that rituals naturally benefit from. Or at least it doesn\u2019t take a mage much to make sure they apply. Meanwhile fast casting is an automatic +1 unless the mage has practiced the spell (like if it\u2019s a rote or he\u2019s cast it several times in play).<br /> <br /> Also, two things about time. Simple spells are not always a roll per round, and rituals are not always long drawn out affairs. Yes there are recommendations for how long rituals take but ultimately the time should be based on magical style and what is dramatically appropriate. Simple spells, like simple rolls, can actually represent minutes or hours of time. And your mileage may vary but in the right situation I\u2019d let mages cast rituals in combat time (though I\u2019d make them make lots of skill checks to avoid messing something up).<br /> <br /> I would recommend trying it out in practice and seeing how each type of roll ends up working out.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-09T04:55:31-07:00","id":1558745,"title":"","content":"<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bComing in late.<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558664#post1558664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only reason that this seems a bit intended was because of revised edition that worked this way. M20 does a terrible job even hinting that way.<br /> Out of curiosity, how does your table rule that?</div> </div> </div> </div>My table uses the rule on M20 p.547, under Vulgar Magick: &quot;Successful Vulgar Magick earns one point of Paradox.&quot; It then goes on to cite a box talking about how MRev featured a more punishing system where the amount of Paradox depends on the highest required Sphere; but I agree with the author's assessment that that's <i>too</i> punishing, and I don't use it. <br /> <br /> That's for the first roll. Subsequent rolls use the rules on pp.540\u2013541: &quot;\u2026every roll after the first one adds one point of Paradox to the caster's total. If the ritual concludes successfully, then those extra points of Paradox go away.&quot; It then speaks about what happens if the ritual is botched, but not what happens if it fails. My reading of it is that you keep the extra Paradox on a normal fail, and double down on it if you botch. <br /> <br /> &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot; isn't a non-Ritual extended roll: <i>all</i> extended rolls are Rituals. What &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot; is is a means of resuming an Extended roll after you've suspended it. (See p.542) The Wits+Esoterica/Technology roll isn't about accumulating successes on the extended roll; it's about whether or not you get to keep the successes you've already accumulated. On a success, you keep them, and your next Arete roll will add to them; on a failure, all previously accumulated successes are lost and your next Arete roll will start things over with a new first roll. <br /> <br /> However, previously accumulated Paradox doesn't go away; it stays in your pool, the price for the failed first attempt at the ritual. And since you're starting over, it no longer counts as Paradox accumulated during the Ritual: it doesn't go away if the next attempt at the ritual succeeds, but it also doesn't effectively double if the next attempt botches. It's no longer associated with the ritual at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-09T07:20:07-07:00","id":1558750,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558745#post1558745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot; isn't a non-Ritual extended roll: <i>all</i> extended rolls are Rituals. What &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot; is is a means of resuming an Extended roll after you've suspended it. (See p.542) The Wits+Esoterica/Technology roll isn't about accumulating successes on the extended roll; it's about whether or not you get to keep the successes you've already accumulated. On a success, you keep them, and your next Arete roll will add to them; on a failure, all previously accumulated successes are lost and your next Arete roll will start things over with a new first roll.</div> </div> </div> </div>Strictly speaking, there are two sections that deal with Picking Up Where You Left Off, one in basic spell rolling rules (pg 537) and one in the ritual rules (pg 542). That said, I agree that the one on pg 537 is best understood not as an extended roll but as an urgent attempt to finish a spell that should have been complete in a single roll and fell short. It's not an intentional situation.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-10T10:49:25-07:00","id":1558770,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> about your post #14: <br /> <br /> Theoretically, every regular roll (plus thebpicking up) should take a lot less than rituals in time. Even if the casting isn't an action, it most likely wont take 5+ minutes or the game would be very low paced.<br /> <br /> And as you said, there are diff reducers that already part of a prolongued casting, like researching and takin more tine to cast. This last one can give you -3 reduction if you triple the time, so your spell that took about 1 min would bow take 3, which is still way faster than a ritual. On top of that, no big potential-dox risk.<br /> <br /> So, in which occasions are rituals actually better, if you have the meansnto reduce difficulty (didn't even mentioned quint yet)?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558745#post1558745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> \u200b<br /> \u200b<br /> My table uses the rule on M20 p.547, under Vulgar Magick: &quot;Successful Vulgar Magick earns one point of Paradox.&quot; It then goes on to cite a box talking about how MRev featured a more punishing system where the amount of Paradox depends on the highest required Sphere; but I agree with the author's assessment that that's <i>too</i> punishing, and I don't use it.</div> </div> </div> </div> I was talking about if you give paradox to failed vulgar spells or only successful ones.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558745#post1558745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> That's for the first roll. Subsequent rolls use the rules on pp.540\u2013541: &quot;\u2026every roll after the first one adds one point of Paradox to the caster's total. If the ritual concludes successfully, then those extra points of Paradox go away.&quot; It then speaks about what happens if the ritual is botched, but not what happens if it fails. My reading of it is that you keep the extra Paradox on a normal fail, and double down on it if you botch.</div> </div> </div> </div> That's the rule for rituals, not the &quot;picking up&quot; thing. But yeah, it doesn't tell what happens to the potential paradox on a regular fail. It isn't doubled on botches, you just get all the potential dox accumulated. For that, I believe a regular fail would not grant the potential paradox (just like a success).<br /> <br /> A middle ground would be to give half the accumulated dox, but that's not raw at all.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558745#post1558745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot; isn't a non-Ritual extended roll: <i>all</i> extended rolls are Rituals. What &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot; is is a means of resuming an Extended roll after you've suspended it. (See p.542) The Wits+Esoterica/Technology roll isn't about accumulating successes on the extended roll; it's about whether or not you get to keep the successes you've already accumulated. On a success, you keep them, and your next Arete roll will add to them; on a failure, all previously accumulated successes are lost and your next Arete roll will start things over with a new first roll.<br /> <br /> However, previously accumulated Paradox doesn't go away; it stays in your pool, the price for the failed first attempt at the ritual. And since you're starting over, it no longer counts as Paradox accumulated during the Ritual: it doesn't go away if the next attempt at the ritual succeeds, but it also doesn't effectively double if the next attempt botches. It's no longer associated with the ritual at all.</div> </div> </div> </div> You are mixing up two different rules. &quot;Picking up where you left off&quot; are for regular rolls and is not considered an extended nor ritual casting. I made an argument about that in this very thread.<br /> <br /> The one you are talking about is for tsking breaks on rituals, which is a different rule from thebone this thread is about<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-10T13:46:13-07:00","id":1558772,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Theoretically, every regular roll (plus thebpicking up) should take a lot less than rituals in time. Even if the casting isn't an action, it most likely wont take 5+ minutes or the game would be very low paced.<br /> <br /> And as you said, there are diff reducers that already part of a prolongued casting, like researching and takin more tine to cast. This last one can give you -3 reduction if you triple the time, so your spell that took about 1 min would bow take 3, which is still way faster than a ritual. On top of that, no big potential-dox risk.<br /> <br /> So, in which occasions are rituals actually better, if you have the meansnto reduce difficulty (didn't even mentioned quint yet)?</div> </div> </div> </div>If you can only reduce your difficulty to -3, then Rituals would be always better if you have the time. It's better to cast at -3 than to cast at -3, -2, -1, 0 ...<br /> <br /> Mechanically rituals are better when the amount of rolls you will have to make can't be &quot;tanked&quot; by your means to reduce the difficulty, AND you have the time. It depends a lot on how many successes you need and how much Arete you have.<br /> <br /> *A &quot;brief rite&quot; (up to 5 successes) takes 5 minutes <i>tops</i>. So, in this case there's no reason to think a ritual would take longer (could easily allow one roll per turn)<br /> <br /> *&quot;Ceremonies&quot; will almost always take longer and I think are at the soft spot where this <i>could </i>lead to abuse because up to 10 successes aren't <i>that</i> hard to get on a few rolls, if you have a lot of modifiers on your favor. Even then, if you have the hours to spend I think it's still better to not risk several rolls and pile up difficulties.<br /> <br /> *A &quot;Great Work&quot; most certainly takes longer, but it's also for stuff that requires a lot of successes (and here's where cults and working in concert shine). For most cases it wouldn't be wise to try to &quot;fast-cast&quot; this sort of stuff, even if it wasn't completely against the spirit of the rule. But there could be some fringe cases where &quot;picking up&quot; could be better...if it's allowed <hr /><br /> Let's not forget that the &quot;Picking Up Where You Left Out&quot; rule itself asks from the ST wheter a Ritual migth be in order after the first retry. So, RAW, the ST <i>has </i>to adjudicate wheter you can use one or the other. It's not a &quot;default option&quot; you can choose to do w/o asking the ST.<br /> <br /> RAI, it's likely intended for things you can reasonably do in one roll (hence why it asks you if a Rituals shouldn't be in order after the first retry, if your retry wasn't a Failure).<br /> <br /> Mage isn't a bulletproof game, but I think this one ain't so problematic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":".  Minnor correction 03-10-2026, 04:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-15T13:05:13-07:00","id":1558876,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558772#post1558772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> If you can only reduce your difficulty to -3, then Rituals would be always better if you have the time. It's better to cast at -3 than to cast at -3, -2, -1, 0 ...<br /> <br /> Mechanically rituals are better when the amount of rolls you will have to make can't be &quot;tanked&quot; by your means to reduce the difficulty, AND you have the time. It depends a lot on how many successes you need and how much Arete you have.</div> </div> </div> </div> Indeed. But having time gives a lot of good modifiers, like researching (up to -3). Taking more time also can give up to -3. If you can find a node, uo to -3 too.<br /> <br /> So you can get about easily -6 and possibly -9 or better (Quintesence, etc). Which means that if you plan to roll less than those amounts, then your diff would not increase and picking up is still better ,regarding time and paradox, than rituals. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558772#post1558772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Let's not forget that the &quot;Picking Up Where You Left Out&quot; rule itself asks from the ST wheter a Ritual migth be in order after the first retry. So, RAW, the ST <i>has </i>to adjudicate wheter you can use one or the other. It's not a &quot;default option&quot; you can choose to do w/o asking the ST.</div> </div> </div> </div> <br /> It doesn't ask the ST, it asks the reader. It is still a ST decision as much as everything in the game is, what I mean is that it isn't explicitly an optional rule or ST call.<br /> Also, for what reason wouldn't the ST allow the character to keep trying something? <br /> <br /> Yeah, it's not a game breaker, but I believe rituals should be an option way better mechanically\u2026 <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-16T07:58:51-07:00","id":1558891,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558876#post1558876\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah, it's not a game breaker, but I believe rituals should be an option way better mechanically\u2026<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I think the largest part of this is that we are just approaching this from different angles and assumptions so my natural conclusions seem arbitrary to you, and your natural conclusions look like an exploit to bypass rituals to me. I say that to cast something as a basic/simple spell (however you want to say it) we have to have a reasonable belief that it can be done in one action/roll and the 'picking up' rules are just there for when the dice work against that. So a player can't intentionally use the 'picking up' rules to cast an extended spell. I think that is something we can reason out from the way simple actions and extended actions work.<br /> <br /> But... nowhere in the magic rules does it directly say that. So you see (or seem to see, let me know if I am getting you wrong) that using the 'picking up' rules is something a player (and narratively, the mage) can choose to do deliberately. You see two different ways of approaching extended rolls that the player can frequently choose between and therefore can mechanically compare.<br /> <br /> Does that sound right to you?<br /> <hr /><br /> To follow up with one other observation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">And as you said, there are diff reducers that already part of a prolongued casting, like researching and takin more tine to cast. This last one can give you -3 reduction if you triple the time, so your spell that took about 1 min would bow take 3, which is still way faster than a ritual. On top of that, no big potential-dox risk.</div> </div> </div> </div>This, I think, is another point where we are just looking at things differently. If a mage is extending rolls to the point that they are taking a minute per cast (let alone 3 minutes) and there is at least a reasonable chance of having to roll more than once... why wouldn't that just be a ritual. As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a> said, the time frame suggestion for a simple rite is 5 minutes max, so you are into the same time range as a ritual anyways.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-16T16:33:19-07:00","id":1558903,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> There is no reason to prohibit picking up rule. This and rituals are pretty different from an in-game perspective. A ritual would probably involve a lot of preparation, while picking up feels like a regular casting. Still, limiting on or the other based on successes needed just doesn't feel right. I'd rather have a mechanical reason.<br /> I mean the character can always try again and prohibiting that seems wrong to me","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-16T18:30:40-07:00","id":1558905,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558903#post1558903\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> There is no reason to prohibit picking up rule. This and rituals are pretty different from an in-game perspective. A ritual would probably involve a lot of preparation, while picking up feels like a regular casting. Still, limiting on or the other based on successes needed just doesn't feel right. I'd rather have a mechanical reason.<br /> I mean the character can always try again and prohibiting that seems wrong to me</div> </div> </div> </div>This is what I mean about different perspectives; I\u2019m not banning that rule at all (and I\u2019m actually not quite sure why you think I am). It\u2019s just that from what I see it has a different purpose than you are using it for.<br /> <br /> You say \u2018picking up\u2019 feels more like regular casting, and it should because \u2018picking up is meant to be used with regular casting <i>when regular casting has gone wrong</i>. It\u2019s a backup system, not the main rule.<br /> <br /> Of course Im aware that\u2019s not what you are seeing, and I\u2019m willing to help you compare the mechanics of the picking up rules and ritual casting. But since I think they are intended for such different situations, I\u2019m not surprised that they aren\u2019t balanced against each other.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-20T11:24:13-07:00","id":1558954,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558905#post1558905\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is what I mean about different perspectives; I\u2019m not banning that rule at all (and I\u2019m actually not quite sure why you think I am). It\u2019s just that from what I see it has a different purpose than you are using it for.<br /> <br /> You say \u2018picking up\u2019 feels more like regular casting, and it should because \u2018picking up is meant to be used with regular casting <i>when regular casting has gone wrong</i>. It\u2019s a backup system, not the main rule.<br /> <br /> Of course Im aware that\u2019s not what you are seeing, and I\u2019m willing to help you compare the mechanics of the picking up rules and ritual casting. But since I think they are intended for such different situations, I\u2019m not surprised that they aren\u2019t balanced against each other.</div> </div> </div> </div> The thing is that anything short of a complete (or extraordinary) success is considered a &quot;failure&quot; and allows for using that rule.<br /> <br /> And I see no logic in not allowing it for spells that require more successes. Let's say you have Arete 1, then anything that affects the outside world would require a ritual?<br /> <br /> Imho, the rules just werent well thought enough and picking up is simply better than rituals. And iirc, that's also true in earlier editions, because I believe the rules were kinda the same?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-23T13:48:08-07:00","id":1558978,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558954#post1558954\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The thing is that anything short of a complete (or extraordinary) success is considered a &quot;failure&quot; and allows for using that rule.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I agree. Any regular casting roll that falls short allows for using that rule. The bigger question is what can a mage attempt to do as a regular casting roll, because there are some things that either narratively or mechanically don't make sense in that context. Narratively some things are just too big to make sense from a storyline perspective, and mechanically, some things are too unlikely to succeed as regular rolls to drag down the game with. At some point the sheer number of rolls can start dragging down the action of the game.<br /> <br /> I'll add that in dire situations where the characters do not have time or space to set up a ritual and where the drama of the dice rolling is enhancing the scene rather than dragging it down, I would allow players to use the regular casting rules for spells that in most other cases wouldn't qualify. Narrative matters a lot. <br /> <br /> Now I'm not saying that there's a clear line that separates what can only be done with ritual from what can be done with regular casting. That decision is largely up to the ST and the rest of the table. But for me, I'd say that if I were looking at a situation where casting as a standard spell with pickup rules would take an average of 14 rolls and 2 restarts (because of botching), while the same spell ritually cast would take an average of 7 rolls with no restarts, I'd say it doesn't make much sense as a standard spell.<br /> <br /> Those numbers didn't come from nowhere, by the way, those are the numbers for a Arete 3, Rank 3, 5 success coincidental spell without considering modifiers. That's only two successes more than the dice pool. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Let's say you have Arete 1, then anything that affects the outside world would require a ritual? </div> </div> </div>What did you have in mind? Because like I said at least some of this is situational.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-24T09:02:06-07:00","id":1558992,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558978#post1558978\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The bigger question is what can a mage attempt to do as a regular casting roll, because there are some things that either narratively or mechanically don't make sense in that context. Narratively some things are just too big to make sense from a storyline perspective, and mechanically, some things are too unlikely to succeed as regular rolls to drag down the game with. At some point the sheer number of rolls can start dragging down the action of the game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, anything. If the mage wants to try and jump over a bus or lift a car, he can try it, even though (without good magick buffs) he will probably fail.<br /> <br /> The thing is, the game predicts and allows trying to cast a 5 success spell with only Arete 3, even if not using a ritual. Getting 3 sux on that would make an incomplete spell, but one that would string bring results. So saying, &quot;nah, you can't cast that spell because it requires more successes than your Arete rank&quot; isn't fair, not part of the raw. Of course, the ST is free to do that, but that is not what I'm discussing in this thread.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558978#post1558978\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll add that in dire situations where the characters do not have time or space to set up a ritual and where the drama of the dice rolling is enhancing the scene rather than dragging it down, I would allow players to use the regular casting rules for spells that in most other cases wouldn't qualify. Narrative matters a lot.<br /> <br /> Now I'm not saying that there's a clear line that separates what can only be done with ritual from what can be done with regular casting. That decision is largely up to the ST and the rest of the table.</div> </div> </div> </div>As far as I know, there is no line that separates regular casting and rituals. Everything you can do with one you can do with the other (of course, considering the respective instruments and foci, etc). There is no rule that says X needs a ritual and Y is a regular casting.<br /> <br /> All the book says is that sometimes rituals are more convenient and my whole argument is that they are not, as long as you can deal with some modifiers.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558978#post1558978\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But for me, I'd say that if I were looking at a situation where casting as a standard spell with pickup rules would take an average of 14 rolls and 2 restarts (because of botching), while the same spell ritually cast would take an average of 7 rolls with no restarts, I'd say it doesn't make much sense as a standard spell.<br /> <br /> Those numbers didn't come from nowhere, by the way, those are the numbers for a Arete 3, Rank 3, 5 success coincidental spell without considering modifiers. That's only two successes more than the dice pool.</div> </div> </div> </div>Are you saying that you need 14 rolls (42 dices) to achieve 5 successes at diff 6? That seems wrong. But anyway, you are not taking into account difficulty reductions. If you do the math again, now considering that you can lower the diff to 3, and keep it at 3 for a few rolls, you'll see that regular casting in fact are better than rituals, and thus makes more sense.<br /> <br /> Maybe that is exactly what they consider to be the con of the regular casting, the dire need to reduce difficulty to be useable this way. Or maybe it's just a rule bug that didn't cross the authors minds that mages can lower difficulties by several ways, idk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-24T11:01:50-07:00","id":1558994,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558992#post1558992\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, anything. If the mage wants to try and jump over a bus or lift a car, he can try it, even though (without good magick buffs) he will probably fail.<br /> ...<br /> So saying, &quot;nah, you can't cast that spell because it requires more successes than your Arete rank&quot; isn't fair, not part of the raw. Of course, the ST is free to do that, but that is not what I'm discussing in this thread.</div> </div> </div> </div>First off, that's simply not true. There are tasks which are just impossible, where even a 10 doesn't count as a success. Mage uses the hyperbolic language of shooting the moon out of the sky, but it doesn't have to be that ridiculous.<br /> <br /> Secondly, if we are going to get technical with RAW there are several places that say that extended magical rolls <i>are</i> rituals, so that would indicate that a roll that the mage <i>can't</i> complete in a single roll has to be done as a ritual.<br /> <br /> Third, we are also starting to get into an area where the needs of running the game determine a lot. There is a point where I will not allow players to waste table time on the obviously unfeasible because doing so will harm everyone's enjoyment of the game... including their own. It isn't <i>fair</i> to stall out the action of a scene while someone tries to spend 14 turns to cast something with a system that's not optimized to handle that.<br /> <br /> If the rules allowed that sort of thing I would <i>have</i> to override them. Fortunately, there are good indications that they don't and we've already gone over several of those indications in this thread.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As far as I know, there is no line that separates regular casting and rituals. Everything you can do with one you can do with the other (of course, considering the respective instruments and foci, etc). There is no rule that says X needs a ritual and Y is a regular casting. </div> </div> </div>If you are looking for a specific, iron clad wording that separates the two, sure, that's not there. But given that the book says extended spell rolls are rituals, and that the picking up section suggests that after one or two extra rolls &quot;an extended roll might be in order,&quot; we can see that there is supposed to be a difference.<br /> <br /> In fact, we can also see that the picking up rules are not intended to be an extended roll, and that they aren't intended to be a replacement for an extended roll. I don't think there's anything <i>stopping</i> you from allowing 20+ picking up rolls (outside the previously discussed ST who wants to keep the flow of the game going) but it sure doesn't look like that's the intended use.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Are you saying that you need 14 rolls (42 dices) to achieve 5 successes at diff 6? That seems wrong. </div> </div> </div>Yes, on average. Because botching wipes successes. And honestly, I was generous in allowing the mage to continue casting after the botch because trying again normally only applies to simple failures. If the mage has to stop after the first botch, he'll botch over 50% of the time. That +1 difficulty each roll matters a lot.<br /> <br /> Sometimes you really have to try these things out because the human mind is not great at intuiting probability.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But anyway, you are not taking into account difficulty reductions. If you do the math again, now considering that you can lower the diff to 3, and keep it at 3 for a few rolls, you'll see that regular casting in fact are better than rituals, and thus makes more sense. </div> </div> </div>I'm not in a position to run the numbers for that today, but you'd need at least -7 difficulty to accomplish that. I'm actually curious what kind of situation and spell you see where the mage can engineer that difficulty modifier, maintain it for as long as necessary, needs that many successes and where the spell either narratively or mechanically doesn't make sense as a ritual. That seems like a very rare situation to me, but maybe my imagination is failing me.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-24T12:10:07-07:00","id":1558996,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558876#post1558876\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All the book says is that sometimes rituals are more convenient and my whole argument is that they are not, as long as you can deal with some modifiers.</div> </div> </div> </div>The book doesn't say that it's more convinient because it's more powerful. As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> says: It could be understood as more convinient because it transforms a boring series of rolls that don't let other players act into a project spanning more of what we could call &quot;a story&quot;<br /> <br /> But, then, you are doing that anyway to get the modifiers aren't you?<br /> <br /> Narrative does suggest that rituals take more time than &quot;picking it up&quot;, yeah, but if you're already &quot;taking more time&quot; to gain a bonus, and &quot;researching the subject&quot; (that could involve a long series of actions in itself), and also poaching expensive and contested resources<br /> <br /> ... then why isn't that a ritual?... Bear that question in mind. <hr />\u200b\u200b<br /> I think the problem here it's the idea that rituals have to &quot;take more time&quot;. And that simply isn't true w/o the <i>optional rule</i> of Great Works. On Revised and below there was no <b>rule </b>saying that you needed to take more time rolling for a ritual than for any normal casting.<br /> <br /> It <i>could </i>take more time, and it's strongly suggested it does, but there was no <i>hard</i> <i>rule</i> about how much. Technically you could make tens of rolls turn by turn and call it a ritual - albeit in practice nobody would for the reasons mentioned above.<br /> <br /> If Great Work rule it's getting in the way, I would suggest ditching it and just ruling time narratively as it was always done.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It doesn't ask the ST, it asks the reader. It is still a ST decision as much as everything in the game is, what I mean is that it isn't explicitly an optional rule or ST call. </div> </div> </div>It's not an optional rule, but it's a rule that has the very explicit option of being discarded after the first roll. The ST, as the final arbiter of the rules, it's the only reader for which that question it's more than a hypotetical.<br /> <br /> If you use the Great Work rules, and are afraid of players making an exploit of the &quot;picking it up&quot; rules, then you can also use that option.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-24T13:15:18-07:00","id":1558997,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558994#post1558994\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Third, we are also starting to get into an area where the needs of running the game determine a lot. There is a point where I will not allow players to waste table time on the obviously unfeasible because doing so will harm everyone's enjoyment of the game... including their own. It isn't <i>fair</i> to stall out the action of a scene while someone tries to spend 14 turns to cast something with a system that's not optimized to handle that.<br /> <br /> If the rules allowed that sort of thing I would <i>have</i> to override them. Fortunately, there are good indications that they don't and we've already gone over several of those indications in this thread.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know why you keep saying it's unfeasible, when you can reduce difficulty using several methods. This fact alone makes it not unfeasible, in fact, makes it a very good option. And will take the same amount of table time as a ritual, as long as you can manage the modifiers.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558994#post1558994\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not in a position to run the numbers for that today, but you'd need at least -7 difficulty to accomplish that. I'm actually curious what kind of situation and spell you see where the mage can engineer that difficulty modifier, maintain it for as long as necessary, needs that many successes and where the spell either narratively or mechanically doesn't make sense as a ritual. That seems like a very rare situation to me, but maybe my imagination is failing me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, you can get -3 by researching lore about subject, which might be quick or take time, but *probably* not as much as a ritual.This will vary from case to case, but its still an option. You can get -3 if you spend triple time (which also wont take as long as rituals that can go for days). You can get -3 if you're near a Node. You can get -1 from affinity instruments. -1 from unique instruments. -1 from dropped instruments. -3 from abilities enhancing magick and the best one -X from quintessence.<br /> And I didn't even mentioned specific powerful merits. Of course you probably wont stack all of those, but getting about -5 should be enough and its not that hard to get -7.<br /> <br /> If you manage to get the diff from 6 to 3 in your example (5 sux Arete 3) you manage to complete the spell in 4 rolls average. That means -6 in diff. And that would only take 4 (12 if you take triple time) &quot;casting turns&quot; (which depends on instruments, but still).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558996#post1558996\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... then why isn't that a ritual?... Bear that question in mind.[</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed. It is a ritual but is not a <i>Ritual</i>. It is an activity that can take time and prep effort (only if you're going with the more time+research modifiers) but it's not a Ritual as the rules says. <hr />\u200b<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558996#post1558996\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bI think the problem here it's the idea that rituals have to &quot;take more time&quot;. And that simply isn't true w/o the <i>optional rule</i> of Great Works. On Revised and below there was no <b>rule </b>saying that you needed to take more time rolling for a ritual than for any normal casting.<br /> <br /> It <i>could </i>take more time, and it's strongly suggested it does, but there was no <i>hard</i> <i>rule</i> about how much. Technically you could make tens of rolls turn by turn and call it a ritual - albeit in practice nobody would for the reasons mentioned above.<br /> <br /> If Great Work rule it's getting in the way, I would suggest ditching it and just ruling time narratively as it was always done.</div> </div> </div> </div>That right there is the core of the issue, I believe. The optional Ritual rules makes them extremely time consuming, dangerous and laborious. First they are too long, anything more than 5 successes will take several hours. You also have to roll stamina to keep going. And you accumulate paradox, which is dangerous.<br /> <br /> I guess the major issue is the optional ritual time rule. If the time of casting wasn't that big, Rituals vs Picking up would be a matter of Paradox vs modifiers. You want to risk getting a lot of dox or you want to spend a lot of quint (for example)?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558996#post1558996\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not an optional rule, but it's a rule that has the very explicit option of being discarded after the first roll. The ST, as the final arbiter of the rules, it's the only reader for which that question it's more than a hypotetical.<br /> <br /> If you use the Great Work rules, and are afraid of players making an exploit of the &quot;picking it up&quot; rules, then you can also use that option.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would disagree. Imho, seems like an advice for the player. And the player is the one that will be choosing which action takes place. But this is not important, let's agree to disagree.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-26T07:08:36-07:00","id":1559030,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558997#post1558997\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(which also wont take as long as rituals that can go for days)</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure if you meant this hyperbolically or seriously, but I think that it illustrates one of the major issues here so I'm going to tackle it. Do you think it make sense for a spell to take days as a ritual but only turns as a simple cast?<br /> <br /> If you don't then by using this exploit you are letting rules quirks push the story in strange and nonsensical ways. This is like the old 3rd Edition D&amp;D Great Cleave(I think that's the feat) exploit where if you had a line of goblins stretching across the continent you could travel thousands of miles in a single turn. Even if the rules said that was possible, it doesn't make sense.<br /> <br /> I'd say that it is a good indication that you are pushing the 'picking up' rule well past what it is intended for. But even if you can't accept that, you still shouldn't let them be used in that circumstance. Instead, it is the story and the realness of the game world (verisimilitude) of the game world that should be the final say.<br /> <br /> In that light I'd add that many of the modifiers you are using, such as taking extra time, carefully setting up where the spell is cast, rolling ability checks (and thus taking specific actions), and extra research, are all pushing this towards something that sounds a lot like a ritual.<br /> <br /> If the character is acting like this is a ritual, why is it not? And if your answer is time then I have a more important question:<br /> <br /> <u>Why are you treating the suggested ritual times as ironclad law, but ignoring the suggested cap on picking up rolls?</u><br /> \u200b<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-26T17:37:02-07:00","id":1559036,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a><br /> <br /> Yeah, those are all good points. And yes it was Great Cleave lol XD. But this D&amp;D rule was clearly a rule flaw. It should have a limit of how many times it could be possible to use the feat in a single turn.<br /> <br /> But then, we are agreeing that the subject of this thread is also a flaw in thr ritual rules.<br /> <br /> Imho, I'm not pushing the picking up rule, it's the ritual rules (including the non-optional) that are not well thought. <br /> <br /> So, to answer your questions:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Do you think it make sense for a spell to take days as a ritual but only turns as a simple cast? </div> </div> </div>Maybe, depends on the situation. If you have higher risk and higher effort to cast it in a shorter time it's ok. It's a matter of choosing if you want to cast a spell with tranquility and spending more time in a secure and energy (quint) free way as a ritual, or if you want to cast it quickly, but risking failure, paradox and having to spend energy and resources to do it. <br /> The issue is that the rules do not work exactly this way. I'd say that for that to happen rituals shouldn't take that huge time (wich we know it might not, since the time rule is optional), and shouldn't risk that much paradox (potential dox).<br /> <br /> So it would be: you want to work/spend energy to get modifiers or do you want to take (a bit) more time? Nothing else should be different (no extra dox nor stamina rolls for rituals).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If the character is acting like this is a ritual, why is it not? </div> </div> </div>Because those things are not part of the foci at all. Let's say the mage instrument for a healing spell is dance. He can make it a magickal ritualistic dance that takes 1 hour of dancing (Ritual, no modifiers). Or he can research about the harm he is going to heal so it can be more effective and do it on a node and get -6 diff from that. It's completely different. <br /> the only modifier that is slightly similar is the &quot;take extra time&quot; but it still isn't the same.<br /> <br /> That, considering it's the same instrument, which is not necessarily true. That mage might use lomg dances for Ritual spells, but vocalization for regular castings. So in-game they are two very diferent ways of doing the effect.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why are you treating the suggested ritual times as ironclad law, but ignoring the suggested cap on picking up rolls? </div> </div> </div>Because the ritual time is presented as a rule (even though optional) and the &quot;cap&quot; is just a single sentence<br /> &quot;<i>For situations in which you try again due to a failed roll \u2013 or try again and fail the roll \u2013 check out the Failed Rolls entry below. In other cases, an extended roll might be in order\u2026 and for those situations, again see the Rituals, Rolls, and Extended Successes entry below</i>&quot;<br /> <br /> It is not presented as a rule, doesn't feel like a cap and doesn't even mention the ST<br /> <br /> It doesn't even makes much sense. It says that extended (Ritual) rolls <b>might</b> be in order in situations when you do not fail the roll. So if it's a failed roll, then use picking up? <br /> <br /> Still, I really appreciate all the answers and insights. My initial doubt was about how picking up rule worked, as it could be interpreted as similar to rituals <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-27T05:43:37-07:00","id":1559040,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It doesn't even makes much sense. It says that extended (Ritual) rolls <b>might</b> be in order in situations when you do not fail the roll. So if it's a failed roll, then use picking up?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, that part it's part of why I think about this rule as intended for stuff you could reasonably do on a single roll. If it's a failed roll, that just means you were very unluky. But if you keep being successful, and yet you're <i>still </i>off the mark, then probability it's that you're attempting something that's not really among the things you could reasonably do on one roll.<br /> <br /> But it's true that the authors should have clarified this, if it was their intention.<br /> <br /> Extended rolls on Mage were always supposed to be rituals, and on 2nd edition there was no &quot;pick it up&quot; rule. If I'm not mistaken, &quot;picking it up&quot; it's a rule from Revised, and on Revised everything took a lot of successes because of the divide-successes rule. So I imagine it was a necessary rule or you would fail at simple magick all the time. It sort of balances itself.<br /> <br /> M20 reduced the numbers of successes needed to cast simple magick, and introduced harsh time rules to curb the exploit of rituals, but forgor to similarly restrict &quot;picking it up&quot;. Even so, rituals are necessary to benefit from Cults and Acting in Concert, so they're still the &quot;state of the art&quot; for trully powerful magicks.<br /> <br /> But it's not a big deal. If you ignore the optional rule, and allow unlimited &quot;pick ups&quot;, it just means there's two ways to cast powerful magick each one with it's own pros and cons.<br /> <br /> You can still limit the amount of rolls per turn to avoid clogging the story thanks to how the casting time isn't fix, and if the player wants to &quot;take time&quot;, study and use a ritual space (a Node), he's sort of doing it already for u. If they instead spend Quintessence, that's not something they can do indefinitely. It rewards players that have resources, power and means with a relatively faster casting time and less risks.<br /> <br /> It's only going to be a problem if a player has trully outrageous amounts of resources to quikly reduce their difficulties (Like, Avatar 5 and enough sources of Quintessence to spend each turn, specialist or/and surpassed instruments, Ressonance, Merits ... It CAN get ridiculous enough that a talk with the ST starts to be a must, in my opinion)<br /> <br /> It's something to be pondered.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":".  Clarification 03-27-2026, 08:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-27T08:32:37-07:00","id":1559042,"title":"","content":"I'm kind of torn here, because there's a lot I want to follow up on here, but I also think there's a core point to be made, which you touch on in your last line. So let me tackle that before moving on.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559036#post1559036\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Still, I really appreciate all the answers and insights. My initial doubt was about how picking up rule worked, as it could be interpreted as similar to rituals</div> </div> </div> </div>It really does seem like most of your problems come when we try to use 'picking up' as an intentional extended rolls. Given that, and given other elements already brought up in this thread (extended rolls are rituals, suggestions of how long 'picking up' goes for, etc.). I'd say we have very good indication that we shouldn't do that.<br /> <br /> Now there are some circumstances where, due to something like desperation or necessity, it does make sense to try a standard casting roll rather than a ritual, but this should be an exception driven by specific circumstances. It shouldn't be driven by mechanics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559036#post1559036\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Imho, I'm not pushing the picking up rule, it's the ritual rules (including the non-optional) that are not well thought.</div> </div> </div> </div>Looking back through the thread, you mostly are focused on how rituals and 'picking up' compare. Can you refresh me on what part of the ritual rules themselves you have a problem with?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559036#post1559036\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because those things are not part of the foci at all. Let's say the mage instrument for a healing spell is dance. He can make it a magickal ritualistic dance that takes 1 hour of dancing (Ritual, no modifiers). Or he can research about the harm he is going to heal so it can be more effective and do it on a node and get -6 diff from that. It's completely different. the only modifier that is slightly similar is the &quot;take extra time&quot; but it still isn't the same.<br /> <br /> That, considering it's the same instrument, which is not necessarily true. That mage might use lomg dances for Ritual spells, but vocalization for regular castings. So in-game they are two very diferent ways of doing the effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>So I don't tend to view it this way. Even if, to use your example, a mage tends to dance when they have the time and sing when they don't that doesn't limit dance to rituals and singing to fast casting. I bring this up because you point out a really good case where a mage might find himself in a place where he has enough modifiers and not enough time, so a quick and dirty fast cast is a better option than setting up a ritual.<br /> <br /> But... this is not really something he can deliberately engineer for the purpose of using the standard cast + picking up rule. If a mage has time to prepare and plan out how he's going to cast, thats pushing towards ritual. Considering all of the modifiers and deliberately only using the ones that don't sound like ritual is fun as an intellectual exercise, but it doesn't make sense in character.<br /> <br /> So if the idea is that the mage knows modifiers like Loci, resonance, etc are all going to be in place, but there's not much time to prepare so he just has to get in, cast as fast as possible, and (optionally) get out, well then I agree with you that is a pretty good case for using standard casting and picking up.<br /> <br /> But if the mage spends any time planning out his spell, he's essentially preparing a ritual. Even if he's practicing using his faster foci, or researching non-magical information about the effect, narratively that is still preparing a ritual. All of that pushes towards casting a ritual <i>with shorter casting rolls</i>, not using a standard casting roll with pickup rules.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-27T21:39:04-07:00","id":1559049,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559040#post1559040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Extended rolls on Mage were always supposed to be rituals, and on 2nd edition there was no &quot;pick it up&quot; rule. If I'm not mistaken, &quot;picking it up&quot; it's a rule from Revised, and on Revised everything took a lot of successes because of the divide-successes rule. So I imagine it was a necessary rule or you would fail at simple magick all the time. It sort of balances itself.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, there was.Page 170 Degrees of Success says you can keep trying on a Total or Partial failure with +1 diff. It didn't had that &quot;Picking Up&quot; name, but the rule was there. Same for Revised. And bot hdo not have that *advice* M20 gives, it's just stated as an option. That's another reason to believe it's not up to the ST.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559040#post1559040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But it's not a big deal. If you ignore the optional rule, and allow unlimited &quot;pick ups&quot;, it just means there's two ways to cast powerful magick each one with it's own pros and cons</div> </div> </div> </div>That's true. My issue is that of both options, Piking Up seems a lot better if you have some ways of lowering difficulty<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559040#post1559040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You can still limit the amount of rolls per turn to avoid clogging the story thanks to how the casting time isn't fix, and if the player wants to &quot;take time&quot;, study and use a ritual space (a Node), he's sort of doing it already for u. If they instead spend Quintessence, that's not something they can do indefinitely. It rewards players that have resources, power and means with a relatively faster casting time and less risks.<br /> <br /> It's only going to be a problem if a player has trully outrageous amounts of resources to quikly reduce their difficulties (Like, Avatar 5 and enough sources of Quintessence to spend each turn, specialist or/and surpassed instruments, Ressonance, Merits ... It CAN get ridiculous enough that a talk with the ST starts to be a must, in my opinion)<br /> zz0.12wnq80abbuzz</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, but as I said before, quinte is not the only way to lower difficulties. If you use afinity instruments, a merit and Abilities enhance magick, you already have enough to work with Picking Up. I grant that those are not certain (as you relly on dice rolls), though. Maybe I'm taking the modifiers too much as granted...<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559042#post1559042\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It really does seem like most of your problems come when we try to use 'picking up' as an intentional extended rolls. Given that, and given other elements already brought up in this thread (extended rolls are rituals, suggestions of how long 'picking up' goes for, etc.). I'd say we have very good indication that we shouldn't do that.<br /> <br /> Now there are some circumstances where, due to something like desperation or necessity, it does make sense to try a standard casting roll rather than a ritual, but this should be an exception driven by specific circumstances. It shouldn't be driven by mechanics.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why? Regular casting isn't necessarily a desperate scenario. You can have al the time in the world and try a regular cast, which didn't failed, and keep trying with Piking Up. I don't understand why you keep saying that this is a desperate, specific scenario..<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559042#post1559042\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200b<br /> Looking back through the thread, you mostly are focused on how rituals and 'picking up' compare. Can you refresh me on what part of the ritual rules themselves you have a problem with?</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure. My issue mainly the time reqired (although it's an optional rule, but still), the potential paradox and the stamina rolls, that Picking Up does not have. The increasing difficulty seem to be a better choice than those 3, since we can lower with modifiers easily.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559042#post1559042\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200b\u200b<br /> But... this is not really something he can deliberately engineer for the purpose of using the standard cast + picking up rule. If a mage has time to prepare and plan out how he's going to cast, thats pushing towards ritual. Considering all of the modifiers and deliberately only using the ones that don't sound like ritual is fun as an intellectual exercise, but it doesn't make sense in character.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why? Why if the player (and character) set up to cast a spell using modifiers and a quicker casting/instrument why they can't do that? You're saying that every time they cast on a Node and research or take a bit more time it's going to be a ritual and that's not true at all.[/quote]<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559042#post1559042\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> But if the mage spends any time planning out his spell, he's essentially preparing a ritual. Even if he's practicing using his faster foci, or researching non-magical information about the effect, narratively that is still preparing a ritual. All of that pushes towards casting a ritual <i>with shorter casting rolls</i>, not using a standard casting roll with pickup rules.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hum..No it's not. A ritual is using an instrument in a specific, longer way. It is not related to casting on a Node or taking a bit more of time or researching the subject. You can't equate modifiers to rituals. For the sake of the argument a mage could only use Quint and lower the mofidiers using Picking Up and that's not the same as casting as a Ritual at all. I don't understand why you keep trying to relate those to Ritual casting, while they are not necessarily the same. In fact, you could use all of those modifiers <b>while</b> casting as a ritual. They are not the same!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-30T10:17:32-07:00","id":1559121,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559049#post1559049\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why? Regular casting isn't necessarily a desperate scenario. You can have al the time in the world and try a regular cast, which didn't failed, and keep trying with Piking Up. I don't understand why you keep saying that this is a desperate, specific scenario.<br /> ...<br /> Sure. My issue mainly the time reqired (although it's an optional rule, but still), the potential paradox and the stamina rolls, that Picking Up does not have. The increasing difficulty seem to be a better choice than those 3, since we can lower with modifiers easily.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Strange to say, but you are doing an even better job than the book at convincing me that the picking up rules are not meant to be used the way you are using them. If, as I keep saying, standard magic rolls are for spells the mage can likely accomplish in a single roll and rituals are for spells the mage likely needs an extended roll for, then we would expect to see rituals have rules for handling the timing and limits of extended rolls and we would expect standard rolls <i>not</i> have those rules (because they would never roll long enough to use them).<br /> <br /> If, on the other hand, standard spell rolls were intended to be an alternative form of extended roll, we would expect them to have some kind of rules for determining timing, roll limits, etc. We don't. It's yet another indication they aren't meant to be used that way.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're saying that every time they cast on a Node and research or take a bit more time it's going to be a ritual and that's not true at all. </div> </div> </div>No, I'm saying that the major difference between a standard roll and a ritual is if we the spell can be accomplished in a single roll or if it requires multiple rolls. That's the key defining element, much like (though not exactly like) the difference between simple and extended non magical rolls. And narratively we can see this as well, picking up is tough and frustrating in a way that continuing a ritual is not: &quot;In story terms, she grits her teeth, regroups, and gives it the old (wizard) college try once again.&quot;<br /> <br /> Really, even the 'get in, cast, get out' extended roll I described is still a ritual, it's just one that is so hasty and rushed that the mechanics of 'picking up' might better capture the desperation and tenseness of the situation. But that's not about exploiting a loophole to bypass the limitations of rituals, it's about representing the difficulty of the situation.<br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-31T19:54:15-07:00","id":1559152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559121#post1559121\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If, as I keep saying, standard magic rolls are for spells the mage can likely accomplish in a single roll</div> </div> </div> </div>I completely disagree with that. There's no reason to limit spells that require more than the mage's Arete in successes to rituals.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559121#post1559121\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, I'm saying that the major difference between a standard roll and a ritual is if we the spell can be accomplished in a single roll or if it requires multiple rolls. That's the key defining element, much like (though not exactly like) the difference between simple and extended non magical rolls.</div> </div> </div> </div>That was never the difference between them. At least I've never seen this description on any Mage book. The key defining element is the extended roll. Honestly, that difference is very poorly written in M20, the definition and examples of a ritual there could pretty much be a regular casting with a time/effort demanding instrument...<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559121#post1559121\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And narratively we can see this as well, picking up is tough and frustrating in a way that continuing a ritual is not: &quot;In story terms, she grits her teeth, regroups, and gives it the old (wizard) college try once again.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>The ritual is not literally described this way, but what makes you think that it is not frustrating to loose hours (if not days) of work just to get your magick fizzle and probably get a lot of paradox? And what is exactly the argument here? I got lost.<br /> <br /> I believe at this point it all boils down to if you should allow a player to try to cast something that requires more successes than the mage's arete. I see no evidence to disallow that in raw, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-31T21:38:05-07:00","id":1559156,"title":"","content":"Frankly, all of this sounds like you're trying to twist RAW to get the result that you want rather than respecting the obvious RAI. Obviously you can do whatever you want at your table, but there's no way I'd be persuaded by your arguments as a Storyteller.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-01T07:03:38-07:00","id":1559162,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, there was.Page 170 Degrees of Success says you can keep trying on a Total or Partial failure with +1 diff. It didn't had that &quot;Picking Up&quot; name, but the rule was there. Same for Revised. And bot hdo not have that *advice* M20 gives, it's just stated as an option. That's another reason to believe it's not up to the ST.</div> </div> </div> </div>That one it's an interesting find.<br /> <br /> Still, the game does seem to distinguish between extended rolls as something that requires a ritual. On 2nd edition, page 163 Extended Magic Rolls states that rituals are for magick that can't be accomplished with little difficulty (it even mentions 1 to 5 successes as a what that means).<br /> <br /> Of course that's exaggerated and one could interpret it as a suggestion (even when it isn't worded as one - if only because the system, the way it works, allows more than that on a simple roll). One could interpret that there is a gap between those 5 successes that are &quot;little difficulty&quot; and &quot;so complicated that the caster must take extra time to succeed&quot; ( the given definition of ritual ).<br /> <br /> But the rules also say that the player <i>must</i> make an extended roll on these cases (on the cases where &quot;the caster must take extra time to succeed&quot;). it's not a recomendation, or an advice, because it's mandatory. RAW it's contradictory with other parts where it's states that regular effects can take extra time, but the obvious RAI it's that spells that take too long generally should be rituals<br /> <br /> Of course that was 2nd ed. On M20 it's just an advice. But the more I read the more it seems to be the most obvious RAI it's to not allow one to treat &quot;pick it up&quot; as something you can keep doing indefinitely as if it was an extended roll. <hr />The Degrees of Success table on p.170 it's also weird. I says Total Failure it's 0 successes, and you can keep going. But Partial Succcess it's 50%, and <i>then </i>you can keep going. Then what happens if you get, say, 20% of the successes?. Can you keep rolling? Taking that table at face value it's better to get 0 successes than 1, unless the target was 2.<br /> <br /> I guess what makes most sense it's to round it down and say you can keep rolling. But if you don't the spell does nothing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-01-2026, 11:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-01T13:07:16-07:00","id":1559167,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"25886\" data-vbnamecard=\"25886\">Kharnov</a> what exactly do you disagree and think I am twisting in raw?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559162#post1559162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Still, the game does seem to distinguish between extended rolls as something that requires a ritual.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I'm not 100% sure. In 2E we have this text in the same page you referenced:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most Effects are pretty straightforward - either you succeed or you do not. Examples include changing your own shape, magickal brainwashing, conjuring or creating some being or object out of thin air, or passing through the Gauntlet and into the Umbra. Such all-or-nothing magicks demand a certain amount of successes be rolled before the spell takes effect. Simple actions are easy; more complicated ones may take time and effort, i.e., ex-<br /> tended rolls, to complete. The Degrees of Success table handles these cases. </div> </div> </div>And the Degrees of Succeses is the picking up of 2E. So, it seems it is considered an extended roll, but not a ritual. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559162#post1559162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On 2nd edition, page 163 Extended Magic Rolls states that rituals are for magick that can't be accomplished with little difficulty (it even mentions 1 to 5 successes as a what that means).<br /> <br /> Of course that's exaggerated and one could interpret it as a suggestion (even when it isn't worded as one - if only because the system, the way it works, allows more than that on a simple roll). One could interpret that there is a gap between those 5 successes that are &quot;little difficulty&quot; and &quot;so complicated that the caster must take extra time to succeed&quot; ( the given definition of ritual ).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, it's a strange rule, because that would also depend on the mage's Arete. For someone with arete 7, 5 successes isn't necessarily complicated.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559162#post1559162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But the rules also say that the player <i>must</i> make an extended roll on these cases (on the cases where &quot;the caster must take extra time to succeed&quot;). it's not a recomendation, or an advice, because it's mandatory. RAW it's contradictory with other parts where it's states that regular effects can take extra time, but the obvious RAI it's that spells that take too long generally should be rituals</div> </div> </div> </div>As I said, 2E seems to consider picking up as an extended roll (and I think M20 does too, but it's very confusing\u2026)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course that was 2nd ed. On M20 it's just an advice. But the more I read the more it seems to be the most obvious RAI it's to not allow one to treat &quot;pick it up&quot; as something you can keep doing indefinitely as if it was an extended roll. <hr />The Degrees of Success table on p.170 it's also weird. I says Total Failure it's 0 successes, and you can keep going. But Partial Succcess it's 50%, and <i>then </i>you can keep going. Then what happens if you get, say, 20% of the successes?. Can you keep rolling? Taking that table at face value it's better to get 0 successes than 1, unless the target was 2.<br /> <br /> I guess what makes most sense it's to round it down and say you can keep rolling. But if you don't the spell does nothing. </div> </div> </div>I agree with you. To be honest, I believe this would never be really an issue because of the dificulty increase, so you shouldn't be able to do Great Works with that, unless you're very optimized and spent a lot of energy/effort for that. In which case, what's the problem, then?<br /> <br /> I really don't think Ramnesis idea of limiting spells by Arete is rai. And if you don't follow the optional ritual time rule, then you might not even use picking up\u2026<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-01T13:27:12-07:00","id":1559168,"title":"","content":"First of all, I think <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a> and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> have made the arguments better than I ever could. I'm not really here to make a case of my own. But given how the topic keeps carrying on without anyone significantly changing their positions, it seems like the two sides are just talking past each other. I offered an outside opinion to help make it clear that your arguments do not seem rhetorically persuasive to at least some onlookers. And since no one else has come in to support your position, you might want to at least rethink how you're expressing your arguments if you aren't willing to give them up.<br /> <br /> But to me, the RAI is pretty obvious that the &quot;pick up&quot; rule is conditional on failing a single casting roll, while rituals are the RAI for intentional multiple roll casting.<br /> <br /> Your arguments come across as trying to use RAW to give yourself the best of both worlds: the increased successes that come with multiple rolls while avoiding the significant increase in casting time that come with the ritual rules actually intended for that purpose. <br /> <br /> And I won't be persuaded otherwise by appeals to ambiguity or contradictions in the text. We all know the rules are messy in WoD books, Mage especially. I leave debates around the mechanical details to more qualified commenters. I think you need to make a better case for the idea that your position respects the spirit, not the letter, of the &quot;law&quot; being discussed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-02T07:10:11-07:00","id":1559181,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559152#post1559152\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That was never the difference between them. At least I've never seen this description on any Mage book. The key defining element is the extended roll.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because what is an extended roll? Well, we are given the definition in several ways, but one that matters quite a lot is the one on pg 388: &quot;Any activity that involves a number of die rolls before it is completed, with chances for failure coming up several times within that span of time, would be an extended action.&quot;<br /> <br /> So if the key defining difference between a simple and extended roll is multiple rolls with multiple chances for failure, then any situation where we know the task will take several rolls (like, say, the mage's arete roll is smaller than the number of successes and he doesn't have a specialty) is an extended roll and thus a ritual.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559152#post1559152\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The ritual is not literally described this way, but what makes you think that it is not frustrating to loose hours (if not days) of work just to get your magick fizzle and probably get a lot of paradox? And what is exactly the argument here? I got lost.</div> </div> </div> </div>We are comparing continuing a simple casting roll to continuing a ritual, not to the end of the ritual. Falling short on a single ritual roll isn't frustrated because it is expected. That picking up is tough or frustrating enough to add a +1 difficulty indicates it isn't expected.<br /> <br /> <br /> But look, it's apparent you have figured out how you think it works so I'm mostly going to summarize what I've been saying for anyone else who comes across this thread. Here are the elements I see that indicate that the picking up rules for simple casting rolls are not meant to be a form of extended action:<br /> <br /> \u2981 The rules for non-magical simple rolls and extended rolls show what each one is meant to be for, and show what the magical rules are building off of.<br /> <br /> \u2981 There are several statements defining extended casting rolls as rituals and vice versa.<br /> <br /> \u2981 Rituals have a roll limit, simple casting doesn't, which indicates that it isn't intended to make as many rolls.<br /> <br /> \u2981 Rituals have a time limit, simple casting doesn't, which indicates that it isn't intended to go on for very long.<br /> <br /> \u2981 The picking up rules for simple casting directly suggest that if you've have to use them more than once or twice, the spell makes more sense as a ritual.<br /> <br /> \u2981 Picking up is hard/frustrating enough to impose a +1 difficulty even if you roll some successes. Continuing a ritual is not and you only get a +1 if you fail or botch.<br /> <br /> All of that is good indication that picking up is not meant to be used beyond a couple of rolls. The exact limit isn't given directly but we can see some bounds based on dice pool and number of rolls.<br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> Now back in your original post you said you were finding the rules confusing. Since you seem certain what the picking up rules are for it seems we've cleared that confusion up (even if not how I intended). Is there anything else you are confused about?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-02T10:48:22-07:00","id":1559183,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kharnov</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559168#post1559168\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Your arguments come across as trying to use RAW to give yourself the best of both worlds: the increased successes that come with multiple rolls while avoiding the significant increase in casting time that come with the ritual rules actually intended for that purpose.</div> </div> </div> </div>Both options have pros and cons: Rituals take more time and potential paradox, picking up increase diff at each roll.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559181#post1559181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Because what is an extended roll? Well, we are given the definition in several ways&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, that is a great source of confusion, not only in M20, but in earlier editions too.<br /> <br /> As I already posted answering Aleph, 2E has this text:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most Effects are pretty straightforward - either you succeed or you do not. Examples include changing your own shape, magickal brainwashing, conjuring or creating some being or object out of thin air, or passing through the Gauntlet and into the Umbra. Such all-or-nothing magicks demand a certain amount of successes be rolled before the spell takes effect. Simple actions are easy; more complicated ones may take time and effort, i.e., extended rolls, to complete. The Degrees of Success table handles these cases. </div> </div> </div>And the Degrees of Success is the exact table with the &quot;picking up&quot; (although it didn't had this name yet) from older editions.<br /> <br /> Revised had a very similar text, and I quote (page 150):<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most Effects are pretty straightforward - either you succeed or you do not. Examples include hurling a ball of flame, determining the truth with a mind scan or dilating time. Such all-or-nothing magical Effects demand that a certain amount of successes be rolled before the spell takes effect. Simple actions are easy; more complicated ones take time and effort (i.e., extended rolls) to complete. The Degree of Successes table, on the magical reference chart, handles these cases.<br /> (...)<br /> Many Effects also have a possibility of scoring some successes, or of happening partially. In such cases, check the Degree of Success table, and compare it to the required success for the Effect. The Effect may be completed at once, or it may happen only partially. If your mage is shapeshifting but you don't score enough successes to complete the Effect, then you'll only go part way and you'll need to fisnish the spell with an additional Effect. </div> </div> </div>Both editions makes very clear that the picking up rule is considered an extended roll (although not a Ritual). Revised example of shapeshifting is exactly how I pictured it being used: you try to shapeshift (which certainly takes more than 3 successes), but you don't achieve the required successes, so you keep doing it with a following spell. Also note that those quotes were before the Ritual section in both editions.<br /> <br /> Now, M20, for the surprise of no one, mantains tries to mantain those rules, but with different (and more confusing) words.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559181#post1559181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So if the key defining difference between a simple and extended roll is multiple rolls with multiple chances for failure, then any situation where we know the task will take several rolls (like, say, the mage's arete roll is smaller than the number of successes and he doesn't have a specialty) is an extended roll and thus a ritual.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, it's very hard (for the reason you said yourself) to define what is considered an extended roll. I believe a good definition would be: any time you roll more than once and keep your successes.<br /> However, not all extended rolls are considered Rituals, as I showed in 2E and Revised quotes.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559181#post1559181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We are comparing continuing a simple casting roll to continuing a ritual, not to the end of the ritual. Falling short on a single ritual roll isn't frustrated because it is expected. That picking up is tough or frustrating enough to add a +1 difficulty indicates it isn't expected.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure why being an extended roll or not and beng expected or not matters at all, but here we go...Why can't it be expected? The shapechanging example is one which would be expected to not be acomplished in a single roll (unlesss you're a Master).<br /> <br /> The mage does not think &quot;Oh, I have Arete 3 and this effect requires 4 successes, so I might not use vocalization, instead I will dance 1h in a Ritual.&quot;. In-game, he is simply trying to use his quick instrument (vocal) to acomplish an Effect. Just like you can pray to get rich in real life, most people won't think &quot;I need to pray for at least two hours for this to be possible&quot;.<br /> <br /> <br /> About your points:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559181#post1559181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u2981 The rules for non-magical simple rolls and extended rolls show what each one is meant to be for, and show what the magical rules are building off of.</div> </div> </div> </div>I respectfully disagree, because mundane rolls do not have the option to keep going at +1 diff. They have the &quot;Try again&quot; rule, which is not the same as it doesn't carry over successes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559181#post1559181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u2981 There are several statements defining extended casting rolls as rituals and vice versa.</div> </div> </div> </div>Partially agree. As I shown above, all Rituals are extended rolls, but not all extended Rolls are Rituals<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559181#post1559181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u2981 Rituals have a roll limit, simple casting doesn't, which indicates that it isn't intended to make as many rolls.</div> </div> </div> </div>Partially agree. They have an indirect limit in the form of increasing dificulty at each roll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559181#post1559181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u2981 Rituals have a time limit, simple casting doesn't, which indicates that it isn't intended to go on for very long.</div> </div> </div> </div>Idk what you mean by time limit (maximum limit?).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559181#post1559181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u2981 The picking up rules for simple casting directly suggest that if you've have to use them more than once or twice, the spell makes more sense as a ritual.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agree. Indeed it should be safer to make really long casting and several rolls with Rituals, since they do not incerase difficulty.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559181#post1559181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u2981 Picking up is hard/frustrating enough to impose a +1 difficulty even if you roll some successes. Continuing a ritual is not and you only get a +1 if you fail or botch.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agree.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559181#post1559181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All of that is good indication that picking up is not meant to be used beyond a couple of rolls. The exact limit isn't given directly but we can see some bounds based on dice pool and number of rolls.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also agree. Picking up is not <b>meant</b> to be used in long casting rolls, although we have to agree there's nothing stopping you (besides the ST). I believe the limit wasn't given because they trusted the diff increase, but forget all of those juicy modifiers lol.<br /> <br /> I really appreciate your answers and taking your time to give you point of view, though. I would like to make one more question to you: An Arete 2 mage wants to quickly increase his Strength from 1 to 4 in the middle of combat. Would you allow that (using picking up)? Or he would need a &quot;long&quot; Ritual? <hr /><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559181#post1559181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now back in your original post you said you were finding the rules confusing. Since you seem certain what the picking up rules are for it seems we've cleared that confusion up (even if not how I intended). Is there anything else you are confused about?</div> </div> </div> </div>The confusion I had was about how Picking Up should be treated in failures and paradox wise, since M20 is amazingly confusing (and probably that section is just a real mistake). But this thread made me look for answers in earlier editions, and if M20 indeed was to keep the rules from previous editions (which it very often does), then my questions are answered.<br /> <br /> Still, it's really sad the the author simply made a mistake and we should ignore the text because it makes no sense...<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3153,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","user":"Lord Revan","date":". 04-02-2026, 03:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-02T12:39:29-07:00","id":1559185,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3153_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3153\" data-vbnamecard=\"3153\">Lord Revan</a> Mage is full of ways to overcome, or at least balance out, Difficulty penalties, especially when you have the opportunity to plan for it. And there is obviously a huge incentive to plan for it when you have the option to cast larger spells in a timespan measured in rounds rather than the minutes required by even a 'Brief Rite,' as per the optional rules around ritual times.<br /> <br /> Again, explain to me how the argument you're attempting to make does not <b>blatantly</b> disrespect the RAI balance between quick spells cast with a single roll and extended action rituals that sacrifice speed and convenience for an increase in power. Absolutely nothing you say will convince me until you address the fundamental disregard for the spirit of the rules. Or provide <b>absolutely indisputable evidence </b>that your interpretation is completely in line with the intent of the game designers.<br /> <br /> I'd be way more willing to engage with you on matters of crunch if you were putting these ideas forward as your own house-rules with the explicit intention of making Mages more powerful. But I think other posters have made it clear that your interpretation of RAW is dubious at best, and I've made it clear that I find it obviously contradicting RAI. If you can't address the point about RAI, then I won't bother responding again; I've said my piece.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-02T14:49:59-07:00","id":1559189,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559183#post1559183\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure why being an extended roll or not and beng expected or not matters at all, but here we go...Why can't it be expected? The shapechanging example is one which would be expected to not be acomplished in a single roll (unlesss you're a Master).</div> </div> </div> </div>The Shapechanging example, as written in Revised, isn't an extended roll. The second roll is explicitly a second effect. Now that's not really a rebuttal to your argument, because all or nothing effects are also covered and continuing one of those effects would likely be the same effect. The only reason I'm making the distinction is that it matters for the question you ask me later.<br /> <br /> Anyways, regarding Revised, I can't go very far into it because my copy is in storage. I can look up individual pages, though, so I'll just say this before going back to M20: The wording in Revised indicates that it considers spells in the one to five success range to be simple rolls and not rituals. M20, though, includes brief rites (one to five successes) as part of the rituals section, which means spells in that range can be handled whichever way is more appropriate. Now I actually like the Revised approach better because it is much clearer about which rules to use and when. Nevertheless, this is a change between editions.<br /> <br /> One other thing I want to point out is that I've tried (though not always succeeded) to distinguish between intentional extended rolls and unintentional ones. I meant to say last time that picking up is not meant to be an intentional extended action. So yes, the picking up rule can turn a simple roll into a small extended one. That's not really the issue. The bigger question, and the idea you've repeatedly asked about, is if the picking rule can be intentionally exploited to bypass the ritual rules for spells that should be rituals.<br /> <br /> Indeed some - not all, but some - of the examples you've tried have have been quite exploitative of the rules (such as the Arete 3, 15 success example, or the -7 modifier to deliberately avoid casting as a ritual). That's a huge problem in discussing this because there <i>should</i> be an overlap between simple spells and rituals, but how we handle that overlap is very different depending on if we are trying to facilitate play or ward off abuse. So when you say you can't see a set limit on the picking up rules the only thing I can offer is more a more hardline interpretation of the rules. It's not where I'd draw the line in play, but it has more grounding in what is written.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I really appreciate your answers and taking your time to give you point of view, though. I would like to make one more question to you: An Arete 2 mage wants to quickly increase his Strength from 1 to 4 in the middle of combat. Would you allow that (using picking up)? Or he would need a &quot;long&quot; Ritual? </div> </div> </div>This is where the distinction I made earlier with your shapeshifting example matters. This is another case where each subsequent roll is a new effect. So yes, I would allow multiple rolls but each one is a new spell. None of them would count towards the number of ongoing effects, though, since these are Life spells the mage is casting on himself so we can easily consider them locked (locked effects don't count towards number of ongoing effects).<br /> <br /> Now, while that is a legitimate answer, I also know it is a bit of a dodge, so let's take the same situation, but the spell is an Arete 2, 3 success, all or nothing. I'm actually quite flexible when running games, so I would have very little problem allowing a spell that would likely take 2-3 turns to be cast as a simple spell. I also have very little problem allowing rituals with rolls every 1-2 turns, so I could easily see allowing this to be cast as a quick ritual. This isn't likely to run up against the rolling limits for rituals, so the main deciding factor is if I thought the situation warranted the rapidly increasing difficulty rules or not. I probably wouldn't use the Interference rules for such a short spell, but if I did, I'd do it whether it was a ritual or not.<br /> <br /> One other element that isn't really part of the rules but matters a lot in a situation like this: I like to avoid players taking the same action turn after turn. If it looks like that is happening, I look for ways to change the situation in ways they will have to react and adjust to. Nothing major, just something to keep the action from getting stale.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-03T09:35:37-07:00","id":1559201,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"25886\" data-vbnamecard=\"25886\">Kharnov</a> First, I think you need to calm down. No one will provide <b>absolutely indisputable evidence</b> in a system that often contradicts itself and poorly explaim things like Mage. Especially when writtem by a moron that practically did not have editors, like M20.<br /> <br /> I already explained. The balance comes from the increase in difficulty for every roll.<br /> <br /> And there's no interpretation of the rule. They say, in all editions, that you can keep going in the Degree of Successes table. That's it.<br /> <br /> The thing about RAI is that every player have their own. We cannot know for sure the authors intentions, so I prefer to stick with RAW.<br /> Lastly, I do not need to convince nor persuade you of anything. Especially since you haven't provided a good argument of your own and relies on rai and other users answers.<br /> <br /> -----------------------------------------------------------------<br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> Lots of thing to talk about here.<br /> <br /> <b>Extended</b>: Now you got me confused. I was on the idea that Picking Up weren't an extended roll, but then 2E and Revised were very clear that they are, as I quoted in my last post<br /> &quot;Simple actions are easy; more complicated ones take time and effort (i,e.,extended rolls) to complete. The Degree of Success table, on the magical reference chart, handles these cases&quot;<br /> That's from revised, a bit before the shapechanging example<br /> <br /> <b>New effect</b>: I'm not sure those would be considered new effects. Revised and 2E are pretty clear they are the same effect, using words like &quot;Continuing the Effect&quot;. Also, if it were a brand new effect it shouldn't get an increase in difficulty (and the successes would be erased). And for the strength buff example, afaik, multiple buffs do not stack, right? So a new effect would never make strengtg go from 1 to 4 with Arete 2<br /> <br /> <b>Intention</b>: Never in any mage book this rule was related to intention, and honestly, that's the first time I've read this. So, this is up to the ST and not part of RAW nor RAI, at all. Still, I understand that this would be a factor for you.<br /> Now, initially, you said (at least I understood this way), that you wouldn't allow the use of this rule for effects that require more successes than the mage's Arete rank (as you considered them impossible). But you just said you would allow the examples of shapechanging and the buff. Note that for those effects to happen this way, the use of the rule needs to be intentional. Every time a mage casts a spell that requires more successes than his Arete, it's an intentional use of this rule. And since spells often require a lot of successes, this will happen a lot, unless you like to fill the game with rituals or you're playing as Masters.<br /> Personally, I don't care if the player is using it intentionally. For not so small spells, the mage will need to spend resources and effort for it to be at least doable, and I'm ok in rewarding players for that.<br /> <br /> <b>The line</b>: Once again, RAW is not very clear and in every edition it seems more like an advice than a hard rule. 2E and Revised seem to be more clear with the 1 to 5 thing, but even then the wording seem more of a prediction than a rule (if you don't take extra time you won't succeed. Due to the increase in diff).<br /> Now, M20 is way looser, providing no number of successes and uaing words like &quot;might be in order&quot;.<br /> Once again, we're left for a ST decision, with no hard rules to guide us. So, I ask you, where do you draw the line?<br /> <br /> <br /> Imo, the first two (if it's a new spell or extended) are definitions that do not matter. What matters is how it works (intention and the line).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3153,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","user":"Lord Revan","date":". 04-03-2026, 12:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-06T09:40:04-07:00","id":1559247,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559201#post1559201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Extended</b>: Now you got me confused. I was on the idea that Picking Up weren't an extended roll, but then 2E and Revised were very clear that they are, as I quoted in my last post<br /> &quot;Simple actions are easy; more complicated ones take time and effort (i,e.,extended rolls) to complete. The Degree of Success table, on the magical reference chart, handles these cases&quot;<br /> That's from revised, a bit before the shapechanging example</div> </div> </div> </div>As I said, I can't dig through Revised very well right now, so I can't verify how clear. But I have looked up page 150, the one you quoted, and I'd actually say that it has the same problem as M20. The Rituals and Extended Magical Rolls section is written as if extended rolls are rituals, but also indicates that minor feats (1-5 success) range are not. This paragraph from page 150 contains both claims (I'll bold them): &quot;<b>A mage can accomplish most minor effects with little difficulty (one to five successes).</b> However, many effects are so complicated or powerful the caster must take extra time to succeed. In story terms, she has to work some magical ritual; <b>in game terms, you must make an extended roll, gathering enough successes to finish the job</b>.&quot;<br /> <br /> So we would have the same hazy definition problem in Revised as in M20, except that for the rest of that section Revised treats Extended Rolls and Rituals as the same thing which means that as written the increasing difficulty, increasing paradox, and disruption rules apply to all extended casting rolls. So it doesn't matter if there is a technical distinction between simple spells that take multiple rolls and rituals, because the same escalation rules apply to both.<br /> <br /> Possibly M20 intended to say the same thing and that's why picking up says to check out the rules on rituals, but it is such janky writing it is hard to be sure.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559201#post1559201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>New effect</b>: I'm not sure those would be considered new effects. Revised and 2E are pretty clear they are the same effect, using words like &quot;Continuing the Effect&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>The line you quoted specifically calls them new effects:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MRev, p 150</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Many Effects also have a possibility of scoring some successes, or of happening partially. In such cases, check the Degree of Success table, and compare it to the required success for the Effect. The Effect may be completed at once, or it may happen only partially. If your mage is shapeshifting but you don't score enough successes to complete the Effect, then you'll only go part way and you'll <b>need to finish the spell with an additional Effect.</b></div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559201#post1559201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, if it were a brand new effect it shouldn't get an increase in difficulty (and the successes would be erased). And for the strength buff example, afaik, multiple buffs do not stack, right? So a new effect would never make strengtg go from 1 to 4 with Arete 2.</div> </div> </div> </div>Using the M20 rules for picking up, it should. Both imperfect successes and falling short results get the +1. Shapeshifting, as described in the quote, would be an imperfect success.<br /> <br /> That said, Revised doesn't seem to have the rule about +1 difficulty after partial successes. At least, I don't see it on pg 150. If you see it anywhere else, let me know.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559201#post1559201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Intention</b>:<br /> Now, initially, you said (at least I understood this way), that you wouldn't allow the use of this rule for effects that require more successes than the mage's Arete rank (as you considered them impossible). But you just said you would allow the examples of shapechanging and the buff. Note that for those effects to happen this way, the use of the rule needs to be intentional. Every time a mage casts a spell that requires more successes than his Arete, it's an intentional use of this rule. And since spells often require a lot of successes, this will happen a lot, unless you like to fill the game with rituals or you're playing as Masters.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is what you thought my position was at the beginning, but I did correct you on that. Keep in mind, when we were first discussing this, you set up the example of that Arete 3, 15 success roll, and I said that had to be a ritual because there's no reasonable way, mechanically or narratively, that is a simple spell. That wasn't because 15 was greater than 3, it was because of how much greater 15 is than 3. I said that in the very next post I made (<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558509#post1558509\">#5</a>) and I also said that in some circumstances the simple casting rules would work for an Arete 3, 4 success roll.<br /> <br /> My position, which you can see at multiple points throughout the thread, is that the distinction between the two should be both narrative and mechanical, and while standard spells can take more than one roll I would limit it to just a few.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-07T11:06:42-07:00","id":1559276,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559247#post1559247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Rituals and Extended Magical Rolls section is written as if extended rolls are rituals, but also indicates that minor feats (1-5 success) range are not.</div> </div> </div> </div>And, as I posted before already, that same page says that Picking Up are considered extended rolls in that quote:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Simple actions are easy; more complicated ones take time and effort (i.e., extended rolls) to complete. <b>The Degree of Success able, on the magical reference chart, handles these cases.</b> </div> </div> </div>This table is where the Picking Up rule (although it didn't have that name in revised) is presented.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559247#post1559247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So we would have the same hazy definition problem in Revised as in M20, except that for the rest of that section Revised treats Extended Rolls and Rituals as the same thing which means that as written the increasing difficulty, increasing paradox, and disruption rules apply to all extended casting rolls. So it doesn't matter if there is a technical distinction between simple spells that take multiple rolls and rituals, because the same escalation rules apply to both.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think we are talking past each other. Let's try to clarify.<br /> <br /> From Revised page 150:<br /> <br /> 1) The Degree of Success table, where it's said that you can continue the spell for +1 in diff, is used for extended rolls (the quote I just posted above).<br /> 2) All Rituals are extended rolls<br /> 3) Rituals do not increase difficulty on each roll, only failed ones<br /> <br /> From that, we can establish that Rituals aren't the same thing as Picking Up. So, all Rituals are extended rolls, but not all extended rolls are Rituals, being the Picking Up the exception. Do you agree?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559247#post1559247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Possibly M20 intended to say the same thing and that's why picking up says to check out the rules on rituals, but it is such janky writing it is hard to be sure.</div> </div> </div> </div>This here is where my confusion lies, because Picking Up is mechanically and narratively different from Rituals in every edition. So, it they really wanted to change the rules for paradox and failed rolls for Picking Up, it should be clearer and not look like a terrible editing error, as we have now...<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559247#post1559247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The line you quoted specifically calls them new effects:</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, it's an additional effect, but it isn't a <i>new</i> effect. Like casting two fireballs in a row are two effects. You keep your successes, and that's why you get +1 diff. If the mage fails (or get only partial successes) and doesn't want to &quot;Pick Up&quot; in the next turn, but cast a whole new spell from scratch, thus not increasing the difficulty but also not keeping the successes, he could.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559247#post1559247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, Revised doesn't seem to have the rule about +1 difficulty after partial successes. At least, I don't see it on pg 150. If you see it anywhere else, let me know.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is. That's the Degree of Successes table mentioned before. It's on page 209 and says:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> DEGREE OF SUCCESS<br /> <br /> <b>Botch</b>: The mage fails to bend the Tapestry correctly, and the entire Effect comes crashing down. No Effect occurs, and the mage gains Paradox.<br /> <br /> <b>Total failure</b>: No successes, but no botch. Includes successes cancelled by ones rolled or by thresholds. The spell has no effect, yet. The mage may continue, but with cumulative difficulty penalty of one. He may also quit and start over. Paradox accumulates for vulgar Effects just as if the spell had succeeded<br /> <br /> <b>Beginning success</b>: Some successes, but less than 50%. The magic has not formed completely, but it can still be finished properly. Continuing the Effect incurs a cumulative difficulty penalty of one, if desired. If the mage stops now, the Effect has only some minor, trivial result, and Paradox accrues normally.<br /> <br /> <b>Partial success</b>: 50% of the necessary successes. The mystic accomplishes what he set out to do,but not as well as he would have liked. The Effect is incomplete, missing some pieces or in some way flawed. The mage can continue to accumulate successes if desired at an increased difficulty, or he may stop, take the appropriate Paradox and leave the Effect at this sem-functional state.<br /> <br /> <b>Success</b>: 100% of the successes required. The mage does exactly what he wanted to do,<br /> <br /> <b>Extraordinary success</b>: 150% or better. The mage not only succeeds, he succeeds brilliantly. The Effect has a much greater range and strength than he hoped for originally, but not too much to go outside the bounds of the mage's intent. Some all-or- nothing Effects might not have extraordinary results (you did it or you didn't), at the Storyteller's discretion. </div> </div> </div>So, aside from botches (and success, obvisously), the mage can always keep going at +1 diff or stop and try again from scratch (reseting both diff and successes).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559247#post1559247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That is what you thought my position was at the beginning, but I did correct you on that. Keep in mind, when we were first discussing this, you set up the example of that Arete 3, 15 success roll, and I said that had to be a ritual because there's no reasonable way, mechanically or narratively, that is a simple spell. That wasn't because 15 was greater than 3, it was because of how much greater 15 is than 3. I said that in the very next post I made (<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1558509#post1558509\">#5</a>) and I also said that in some circumstances the simple casting rules would work for an Arete 3, 4 success roll.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see. Then intention is not a factor that gets in the way in your opinion, right?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559247#post1559247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200b<br /> My position, which you can see at multiple points throughout the thread, is that the distinction between the two should be both narrative and mechanical, and while standard spells can take more than one roll I would limit it to just a few.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough. But what do you consider as just a few? Where do you draw the line?\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-08T06:43:10-07:00","id":1559288,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559276#post1559276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think we are talking past each other. Let's try to clarify.<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> There is. That's the Degree of Successes table mentioned before. It's on page 209 and says:</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think we are talking past each other on Revised. You have the full book, I don't. So here I'm getting an incomplete picture. That's why I asked if you were seeing things on other pages that clarify what is written on 150. And you did.<br /> <br /> Speaking of which, does anything in MRev contradict the distinction that pg 150 makes that standard rolls are for difficulties in the 1-5 range and rituals are for larger difficulties? That's a very simple and easy to use dividing line.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558454-extended-rolls-and-paradox?p=1559276#post1559276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I see. Then intention is not a factor that gets in the way in your opinion, right?</div> </div> </div> </div>Gets in the way is an odd way of putting it. Intention itself is not the dividing line between standard roll and ritual, but it is related and where the two overlap it does help us determine what is going on both narratively and mechanically. It is a strong indicator, but it isn't the only factor.<br /> <br /> <br /> Fair enough. But what do you consider as just a few? Where do you draw the line?[/QUOTE]<br /> <br /> Two to three in normal circumstances. Maybe more in desperate or rushed circumstances.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}